Proxy ownKeys: the duplicate check and the target-key lookups in LINEAR time

Jahshaka's change, MCP-ON-HOST-1 stage 2 (2026-11-23). quickjs-ng at the pinned
v0.18.0 (d7215b0), file quickjs.c, js_proxy_get_own_property_names.

The cost
--------
A Proxy's ownKeys trap answers a list of n keys; the function then checks it for
duplicates by scanning every earlier key for each key (find_prop_key over
tab[0..i), its own comment: "XXX: inefficient"), and looks each non-configurable
target key up the same way. That is ~n^2/2 comparisons in C, where the runtime's
interrupt handler never runs: a script reaches it with no command at all
(Reflect.ownKeys(new Proxy({}, {ownKeys: () => keys}))). Measured on the
Jahshaka box (Debug, script.host's case 18 before this patch): 32,768 keys 0.25 s,
131,072 keys 3.9 s of CPU (15.6x for 4x the keys); bounded only by the heap
limit - ~0.041 s x (limit in MiB)^2, ~42 s at the MCP host's 32 MiB, ~15 h at a
local host's 1168 MiB - so an outside client could hold the engine's script
thread that long, uncancellable.

The fix
-------
One open-addressing table of the list's atoms (indices into tab, a power of two
at least twice n, linear probing, Fibonacci-multiplied atom): built once in one
pass - a key whose atom is already in the table is the duplicate, found at the
same first index the scan would have found - and used for the target-key
lookups. The list itself is never reordered: the trap's order is the result's.
The table is allocated through js_malloc (it counts against the memory limit; a
refusal is "out of memory") and freed on both exits. find_prop_key, its only
user gone, is removed.

The laws it keeps (script.host case 18, ours): the result in the trap's order; a
duplicate a TypeError "proxy: duplicate property" (at 3 keys, and at index 50,000
repeating index 7); a non-configurable target key the trap leaves out a
TypeError, and an extra key on a non-extensible target a TypeError; the right
lists accepted. Its scaling law: 4x the keys costs under 8x the CPU (~4x now).

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -52789,13 +52789,46 @@
     return res;
 }
 
-/* return the index of the property or -1 if not found */
-static int find_prop_key(const JSPropertyEnum *tab, int n, JSAtom atom)
+/* JAHSHAKA 0001 (irisgl/thirdparty/quickjs-ng-patches): a Proxy's ownKeys list is checked in LINEAR time - an
+   open-addressing table of its atoms beside the list (the list itself never reordered: the trap's order is the
+   result's). The quadratic scans below cost ~2.3e-10 s x n^2 in C where no interrupt reaches. */
+#define JAH_KEY_EMPTY UINT32_MAX
+static uint32_t *jah_key_table(JSContext *ctx, const JSPropertyEnum *tab, uint32_t len, size_t *pmask,
+                               int *pdup)
 {
-    int i;
-    for(i = 0; i < n; i++) {
-        if (tab[i].atom == atom)
-            return i;
+    size_t size = 16, mask, h;
+    uint32_t i, *table;
+    while (size < 2 * (size_t)len)
+        size <<= 1;
+    table = js_malloc(ctx, size * sizeof(table[0]));
+    if (!table)
+        return NULL;
+    memset(table, 0xff, size * sizeof(table[0]));
+    mask = size - 1;
+    *pdup = 0;
+    for (i = 0; i < len; i++) {
+        h = ((uint32_t)tab[i].atom * 2654435761u) & mask;
+        while (table[h] != JAH_KEY_EMPTY) {
+            if (tab[table[h]].atom == tab[i].atom) {
+                *pdup = 1;   /* the first index that repeats an earlier one, as the scan found it */
+                *pmask = mask;
+                return table;
+            }
+            h = (h + 1) & mask;
+        }
+        table[h] = i;
+    }
+    *pmask = mask;
+    return table;
+}
+
+static int jah_key_find(const JSPropertyEnum *tab, const uint32_t *table, size_t mask, JSAtom atom)
+{
+    size_t h = ((uint32_t)atom * 2654435761u) & mask;
+    while (table[h] != JAH_KEY_EMPTY) {
+        if (tab[table[h]].atom == atom)
+            return (int)table[h];
+        h = (h + 1) & mask;
     }
     return -1;
 }
